alan goodman keeps his water bucket  filled to the brim  his home's in the village of everton in  bedfordshire and nearly every week the  taps run dry  you turn the taps on in the morning and  there'd be nothing  uh sometimes you'd turn the taps on and  be low water pressure so not enough to  have a shower  barely enough to fill a kettle maybe if  you if you're lucky  everton is in the driest part of the  country the land here looks bleached and  barren and that's bad news for local  farmers  these carrots they normally would be  nice and firm and standing up and remain  like that three to four days whereas you  can see that they are not too happy  doreen gurney's from a family that's  been farming here for seven generations  she remembers the drought of 1976  but says this year is far worse  so here we are we are in drought  conditions and  we can only irrigate about 25 minutes at  a time and then we have to stop it and  it takes an hour and a half for enough  to build up again  dorian says this summer feels like a  nightmare  the problem here is the village is high  up and it is right at the edge of the  water supply line add a drought into the  mix and you can see why there are real  issues with water  the water company here anglian water  says demand is incredibly high and  there's a risk at peak times pressure  may drop they're currently replacing the  mains to improve  water resilience and if you look at  these satellite images of bedfordshire  you can see how much the area has dried  and what's happening in everton  highlights a wider problem one of water  infrastructure and the need for supply  solutions  we are taking water out of out of our  groundwater and out of our reservoirs  that we really need for the future we're  already quite certain that we're going  to need it next summer and yet we're  we're just kind of taking it anyway as  if the future is some distant place that  we'll never have to go so sure there's  an infrastructure problem but one of the  biggest problems is that we're just not  doing the basics of of of limiting water  usage as quickly as possible now  as the hot dry weather continues yet  more fires this one at east tilbury in  essex the extreme heat has brought  difficult and dangerous risks and across  the country  a collective hope that rain is coming  soon laura bundock sky news everton in  bedfordshire
 

